(project borge-history
 (doc "Git is the history engine. Every agent action gets a commit.")
 (status implemented)
 (section philosophy
  (doc "Why git IS the history system")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The original design had a separate .borg-history/ git repo for
        checkpoints. That was over-engineering. The project already has
        git. Every agent change gets a commit. The commit IS the
        checkpoint. The commit message IS the log. Git diff IS the
        semantic diff engine.

        What makes borge history different from plain git is that borge
        understands the structure of .borg files. It can give you a
        semantic diff (status transitions, section additions, comment
        changes) instead of a text diff. But the storage is still git.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section agent-commit-discipline
  (doc "Agent must commit after every action")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every agent action ends with a git commit. No exceptions. This
        is what makes the audit trail work. If the agent does not
        commit, that is a process violation.

        The commit workflow:
        1. Agent makes changes
        2. Agent runs borge balance + borge check
        3. Agent git adds relevant files
        4. Agent git commits with a descriptive message
        5. Agent runs worktree check (see below)

        If balance or check fails, the agent fixes before committing.
        The commit must never leave the worktree in a broken state.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section worktree-check
  (doc "Verify the worktree is clean and right before declaring done")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Two levels of worktree verification:

        Deterministic (borge check --worktree):
          - git diff is clean (no uncommitted changes)
          - borge balance passes on all .borg files
          - borge check passes on all .borg files
          - dune build succeeds
          - dune runtest passes
          This is pass/fail. CI-friendly. No judgment calls.

        Non-deterministic (borge review):
          - borge lint: any human comment deletions?
          - borge drift: any undocumented functions?
          - Does the commit match what the agent claimed to do?
          - Are there orphaned .borg files?
          This is advisory. It surfaces information for human review.
          It does not pass or fail — it informs.

        Agents run the deterministic check at the end of every action.
        Humans run the non-deterministic review when they want to
        understand what changed.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section semantic-operations
  (doc "Git operations that understand borge structure")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        borge log: git log but with borge-aware summaries.
          Shows only commits that modified .borg files.

        borge diff: git diff for .borg files.
          Highlights status transitions and section changes.

        borge undo: git revert HEAD.
          Convenience command. Current state is preserved because
          revert creates a new commit, not a destructive operation.

        All of these are git operations that understand the borge
        model. No separate storage engine needed.
      |))
  )
 )
)
